  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 99.9307%;\" border=\"1\"><colgroup><col style=\"width: 50.0428%;\"><col style=\"width: 50.0428%;\"></colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Groups&nbsp;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Range (ng/ml)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Male&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&lt; 22.9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non pregnant female&nbsp;</td>\n<td>&lt; 33.6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pregnant female( All trimesters)&nbsp;</td>\n<td>37.1-48298 ng/ml</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pregnancy Associated Plasma Protein - A (PAPP-A ) is secreted by placenta and levels elevate with gestational age. It is also<br>expressed by a variety of tissues, but at much lower levels. Unstable or ruptured coronary artery plaques, but not stable<br>plaques also express PAPP-A. During pregnancy PAPP-A is produced in high concentrations. PAPP-A assessment between<br>11-14 weeks of pregnancy is reported to have significant utility in screening for Down&rsquo;s syndrome and other chromosomal<br>anomalies PAPP-A assessment between 11-14 weeks of pregnancy is reported to have significant utility in screening for<br>Down&rsquo;s syndrome and other chromosomal anomalies</p>\n<p><strong>Increased Levels</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Acute Coronary Syndrome - Unstable angina &amp; Myocardial Infarction.</li>\n<li>Decreased Levels</li>\n<li>Down syndrome - As a marker for maternal screening</li>\n<li>Edward syndrome - Trisomy 18 anomaly</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Coskun A, Serteser M, Duran S, Inal TC, Erdogan BE, Ozpinar A, Can O, Unsal I. Reference interval of pregnancyassociated plasma protein-A in healthy men and non-pregnant women. Journal of Cardiology. 2013 Feb 1;61(2):128-31.</li>\n<li>Kit insert literature</li>\n</ul>",
